[Tournament Analysis on proof ledgers]
  vertices: proof ledgers, not runners
  pairwise observable: lexicographic role score
    (theorem safety, additive locality, product side-channel, obstruction signal,
     magnitude stress, false-positive resistance)
  switch/gauge: larger role score wins; ties use listed Hamiltonian path
  preserves: which quotient can serve which part of the LRC14 proof
  destroys: exact runner geometry and continuous safe intervals
  fingerprint: score_hist=((0, 1), (1, 1), (2, 1), (3, 1), (4, 1), (5, 1)) c3=0 scc=(1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1) hp=1
  Hamiltonian order: q-binding > sum-recursion > Kpq-product > K33-wall > power-stress > raw-iso

[Proof readout]
  The product payload p*q now has a precise job: it is the edge count of
  K_{p,q}.  It must not replace q in the analytic gap, but it can mark
  the incidence complexity of a Farey escape.
  General Farey: F_4 is the first level where the product graph sees the
  Kuratowski K_{3,3} side, via 3/4 -> K_{3,4}.
  LRC14 unit-excess chain: the first nonplanar product graph is 3/41 ->
  K_{3,41}.  That is exactly the S128 near-miss 12->36.
  New local proof split:
    e=0: AP/GW floor candidates;
    e=1,p=1: coarse q-threshold parent 1/13;
    e=1,p=2: planar two-block strip 2/27, needs the petal/two-block kill;
    e=1,p>=3: K3,3 product-minor wall, a three-owner obstruction target.
  The next theorem should not claim nonplanarity is a contradiction by itself.
  It should prove that any remaining q=14 non-AP/GW atom either falls into
  the p=2 two-block strip handled by petal rigidity, or reaches p>=3 where
  the K3,3 ledger supplies the finite three-owner obstruction packet.

